<section class="slide level2">
<h2>What is the Internet?...<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/ITCrowd-1.srt.mp4">1</a>,<a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/ITCrowd-2.srt.mp4">2</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 95%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="image-grid">
<img data-src="../../images/1969_4-node_map.gif" style="max-width: none; width: auto; max-height: none; height:432px"
alt="image" />
<img data-src="../../images/arpanet-4.jpg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; max-height: none; height:432px"
alt="image" />
<img data-src="../../images/imp_log.jpg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; max-height: none; height:432px"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
The first network, the first router, the first message...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The original network diagramming tool was a <span
style="color: blue">pencil</span>, and the <span
style="color: blue">first network had four routers</span> (originally
called <span style="color: blue">IMPs</span> ), and four nodes (At UCSB,
UCLA, SRI and University of Utah). The first successful transmission was
from UCLA to SRI in October 1969, and was recorded in the UCLA
logbook.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Structure of networks now...<span class="hfill-right">
<span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/Intnet1.mp4">9</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 93%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="image-grid">
<img data-src="../../images/networks.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; max-height: none; height:594px"
alt="image" />
<img data-src="../../images/IntNet1.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; max-height: none; height:594px"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Trees and lattices...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The <span style="color: blue">topology</span> of the Internet,
particularly nearer the ’edge’ of the Internet, appears more like a
<span style="color: blue">tree</span> than a <span
style="color: blue">lattice</span>, and we use the <span
style="color: blue">routers</span> to <span
style="color: blue">control</span> access to and from the smaller local
networks. In 2026 there are about 17 billion computers on the
Internet.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Firewalls</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 45%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/DMZLevel1.jpg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; max-height: none; height:540px"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
When a router is protecting you...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Firewalls</span> are brick walls often
found in wooden buildings and are supposed to prevent the spread of
fire. In networking we use the same idea - the <span
style="color: blue">firewall is a router</span>, which limits access to
and from the Internet. We normally imagine that the fire is on the
Internet side :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Systems, networks...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/Internet2.png" style="height: auto; width:70.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Consider the Internet - lots of room for damage
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>No real management except the Internet standards, and seriously
complex.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Internet attack possibilities</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
One of, or some combination of...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Close</span> to you...</p>
</div>
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your WiFi link(s)</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your PCs</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Servers local to your
organization (file, name servers, etc)</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your router(s)</span></li>
</ol>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>But <span style="color: blue">far away</span> too...</p>
</div>
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Routers on the
Internet</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Remote servers that you trust.
For example: DNS for names like gmail.com, ebay, Amazon, Sony PSN, your
bank’s web site...</span></li>
</ol>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Who does all of this? <span style="color: blue">Anonymous
people</span> with (a) a computer, and (b) an Internet connection. <span
style="color: blue">Not too hard.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Internet hacking scenario #1: Malicious DNS</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/dnshack1.png" style="height: auto; width:35.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
A server you trust lies to you...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>You connect to <span style="color: blue">dbs.com.sg</span> to do some
banking.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: magenta">But... A local DNS (Domain name system)
server lies to your computer, and connects you to hackers.r.us who have
put up a spoof version of the bank’s web site. </span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: red">The spoof version collects your password and
login information and proceeds to empty your account.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Internet hacking scenario #2: Malicious website</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/keylog1.png" style="height: auto; width:35.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
A server you trust lies to you...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>A web site <span style="color: blue">fastdownloads.org.uk</span> says
it will<span style="color: blue"> improve your Internet
experience</span> by <span style="color: blue">225%</span>! You say
<span style="color: blue">yes</span>, and it installs a <span
class="dada-emph">useful</span> bit of software.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: red">Actually - it installs something which sends
every key you type to hackers.r.us, and/or allows someone in Guatemala
to control your machine...</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Internet hacking scenario #3: Home Wifi insecurity</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/wifihack1.png" style="height: auto; width:35.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
You overestimate how safe your home is...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Your next door neighbour <span style="color: blue">observes your wifi
</span> network.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: red">Your neighbour hacks your network
authentication password, and from then on, uses your Internet link, and
monitors your traffic to and from the Internet, retrieving your
passwords etc...</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Internet hacking scenario #4: Public Wifi insecurity<span
class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://codebutler.github.io/firesheep/">X</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/starbucks1.png" style="height: auto; width:39.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
You overestimate how safe Starbucks is. You look at facebook...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: red">A cluster of 12 yr-olds there are using
Firesheep, and steal a cookie and take over your facebook session,
posting all sorts of “funny” stuff</span><a href="#/fn1"
class="footnote-ref" id="fnref1"
role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
