<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Internet basics: routing and packets...<span class="hfill-right">
<span>[</span><a
href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc791">ctrl-i1</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/packets.png" style="height: auto; width:60.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Internet traffic sent in packets...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">The original design, specified in the
wonderfully passively named </span><span
style="color: blue">RFCs</span><span style="color: black"> (Requests For
Comments) was for a network </span><span
style="color: blue">resilient</span><span style="color: black"> against
nuclear attacks, to allow the US military to respond to attack from
Russia in the 1960s. No thought was given to </span><span
style="color: blue">security</span><span style="color: black">, only
resilience, and there is lots of opportunity to modify and corrupt
messages; routing information, spoofing etc.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Looking at computer communication</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
A layered reference model for communication...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>A layered model provides a <span style="color: blue">framework</span>
to hang your understanding of computer networking on.</p>
</div>
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
<p>The protocol specifies how</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">communication</span> is performed with the
<span style="color: blue">layer at the same level </span> in the other
computer. This does not for instance mean that the network layer
directly communicates with the network layer in the other computer,
instead each layer <span style="color: blue">uses the services</span>
provided by the layer below it, and <span style="color: blue">provides
services</span> to the layer above it.</p>
</div>
<p>The definition of services between adjacent layers is called an</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">interface</span><span
class="dada-bold">.</span></p>
</div>
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
<p>What are the</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">advantages</span> of the layered
structure?</p>
</div>
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">We can <span
style="color: blue">change layers</span> as needed, the interface
remains constant.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">The networking software is
easier to write, as it has been <span
style="color: blue">modularized</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">The client software is <span
style="color: blue">simpler</span>, as it only needs to know the
interface.</span></li>
</ol>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Note that a <span style="color: blue"><span
class="dada-emph">protocol</span></span><span class="dada-emph"></span>
specifies the <span style="color: blue">interaction between peer
layers</span>. An <span style="color: blue"><span
class="dada-emph">interface</span></span><span class="dada-emph"></span>
specifies the<span style="color: blue"> interactions between adjacent
layers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Looking at computer communication</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/osi-ip.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:4cm"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
The ISO OSI reference model and the IP reference model...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The Internet does not use the 7 layered model. <span
style="color: blue">It only has four layers:</span></p>
</div>
<dl>
<dt><span> IP<span style="color: blue"><span
class="dada-emph"> application </span></span> layer:</span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>for comms between applications on different platforms.</p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span> IP<span style="color: blue"><span
class="dada-emph"> transport </span></span> layer:</span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>for sessions, and network independance.</p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span> IP<span style="color: blue"><span
class="dada-emph"> network </span></span> layer:</span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>for managing routing and addressing.</p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span> IP<span style="color: blue"><span
class="dada-emph"> network-access </span></span> or<span
style="color: blue"><span class="dada-emph"> link </span></span>
layer:</span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>medium, connectors cables independance.</p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Packet (detail) on the Internet</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 50%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/ipencap.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:5cm"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Each layer encapsulates the previous one...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Note that in this way, even if you are just sending a single
character/byte, by the time it is on it’s way, it may be hundreds of
bytes long.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Summary<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc791">ctrl-i1</a>,<a
href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1149">ctrl-i8</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 95%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img
data-src="../../images/Skynet_layout.jpg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:3cm"
alt="image" /></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: red">It seems like we cannot trust the protocols or the
machines!</span></span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img
data-src="../../images/01_img02.jpg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:3cm"
alt="image" /></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
It just got so much worse...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Communication between computers comes in many flavours of <span
class="dada-emph">protocol</span>, and at different levels.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Not only multiple machines that could cause problems, but also
multiple protocols at multiple levels.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>No administrative centre, just general agreement to use the RFCs, not
much in the way of police!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section></section>
<section>
<section id="attacks-and-defences" class="title-slide slide level1">
<h1>Attacks and defences</h1>

</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Outline</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-toc">
<ol>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Networks (Warning - no mathematics
inside)</span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Sources of insecurity - complex
computer systems</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Basics of Internet
traffic</span></li>
</ul></li>
<li><span style="color: blue"><span class="dada-bold">Attacks and
defences</span></span>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-bold">Exploration of attacks</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">IP specific defences - IPv6,
TLS...</span></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Looking at a packet</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 85%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/wireshark3.png" style="height: auto; width:100.0%"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Wireshark displaying an ethernet frame...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Wireshark captures and displays network traffic, not only IP
protocols, but also many of the other communication protocols. You can
inspect the contents of the traffic, broken down by layer.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>“Trust” when using our computer?</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 40%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/doomscrolling.png" style="height: auto; width:100.0%"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Not an exhaustive list by any stretch...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue">Open
the laptop: </span> Use remote services to configure (DHCP, DNS, ARP,
NTP) via routers.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue">Start
a browser with a particular URL:</span> Use remote services (DNS, ARP)
via routers.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Request a web page:</span> Use remote services
(HTTP, ARP) via routers.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Automatic updates:</span> Use remote services (DNS,
ARP, HTTPS) via routers.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue">PKI
certificates: </span> If they are signed they must be good
right?</span></li>
</ol>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>In general <span style="color: blue">we trust </span> each one of
these remote <span style="color: blue">services and routers </span> to
do what we want.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Trust issue #1: ARP</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/arppoison.png" style="height: auto; width:65.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
MAC address for point-to-point communication...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>On Ethernet, when we wish to send a transmission to another host, we
can broadcast it, but normally we use ARP (Address Resolution Protocol),
to find out the specific MAC address for the required IP address. We
then send it to that MAC address, and other hosts will ignore the
transmission.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Trust issue #1: ARP poisoning<span class="hfill-right">
<span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/ARPDNS.mp4">4</a>,<a
href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARP_spoofing">i3</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/arppoison1.png" style="height: auto; width:50.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
Mechanism can be used to subvert any traffic...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Example</span>:</span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>your ARP cache has been <span style="color: blue">poisoned</span>
convincing you that your local router <span
style="color: blue">192.168.100.1</span> is at MAC address <span
style="color: blue"><code>00:11:22:33:44:55</code></span>. This happens
to be a hacking machine on your network. You send your packets addressed
to that machine (which responds to convince you it is <span
style="color: blue">192.168.100.1</span> ).</p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Example</span>:</span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>your router’s ARP cache has been <span
style="color: blue">poisoned</span> to convince it to reply to the
hacking machine instead of your PC.</p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>If your host’s ARP cache has an incorrect value, you will instead
send the frame to the incorrect host.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Trust issue #2: DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/dhcp1.png" style="height: auto; width:65.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Before you do anything...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>When a host’s interface starts or restarts, it sends out a
DHCPDISCOVER broadcast, asking for a nearby authoritative DHCP server to
offer it a useable IP and DNS address, with the DHCPOFFER response.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Trust issue #2: DHCP spoofing</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/dhcp2.png" style="height: auto; width:65.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
Configure host? Sounds easy! What can go wrong?
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>There is no security at all in the process, so maybe something can go
wrong. A malicious DHCP server tells you a <span style="color: blue">bad
router</span> or a bad <span style="color: blue">DNS server.</span> From
then on you send all your DNS requests to this server, and our cached
mapping for <span style="color: blue">dbs.com.sg</span> is
incorrect.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Messin’ with addressin’<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/Everywhere.mp4">3</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 30%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;"><p> </p>
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/ipaddr.png" style="height: auto; width:90.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<p> </p></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><p> </p>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: blue">Application layer: </span> Various (higher
level) protocols, and names (<span style="color: blue">dbs.com.sg</span>
) rather than IP addresses</li>
<li><span style="color: blue">Transport layer: </span> your PC will use
specific ports, with specific (transport-layer) protocols such as TCP or
UDP. (<span style="color: blue">TCP,IP,PORT</span> ).</li>
<li><span style="color: blue">Network layer: </span> your PC will aquire
an IP address(es) (<span style="color: blue">203.127.2.6</span> ) to
identify your machine to others.</li>
<li><span style="color: blue">Link layer: </span> network hardware in
your PC comes with a factory built-in world-wide media access control
(MAC) address. For ethernet<span style="color: blue"> </span><span
style="color: blue"><code>f8:1e:df:e2:b4:63</code></span>. It is easy to
forge MAC addresses.</li>
</ul></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-bubble"
style="background-color: rgb(128,255,128); width: 80%; margin-left: 0; margin-right: auto;">
<p><span>Each layer has its own mode of addressing, with automatic
translations from MAC to network addresses (</span></p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">ARP</span> ), and from names to IP
addresses (<span style="color: blue">DNS</span> ).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Which ports? What services?<span class="hfill-right">
<span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/matrix1.srt.mp4">6</a>,<a
href="https://web.archive.org/web/20120210182541/http://www.cert.org/incident_notes/IN-2001-12.html">i4</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 68%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/nmap-matrix2log-cropped-1.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:5cm" alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
It is just a movie, but you should be paranoid...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Systems are dangerously easy to subvert. And look! nmap!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Consider browsing to a URL </h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/dnshack1.png" style="height: auto; width:70.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
We saw this before...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>When your browser wishes to look at a web-site (dbs.com.sg say), your
machine queries a nearby DNS (name) server. It belongs to a world-wide
system of caching DNS servers, and knows what to do if it does not have
the mapping from name to IP address.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Trust issue #3: DNS poisoning</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
Sounds foolproof, what can possibly go wrong?
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Well - there is little security in the process, so maybe</p>
</div>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Your</span> own <span
style="color: blue">cache</span> could have been <span
style="color: blue">poisoned</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your local <span
style="color: blue">server</span> ’<span style="color: blue">s
cache</span> could have been <span
style="color: blue">poisoned</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">A server <span
style="color: blue">upstream</span> of your DNS could be <span
style="color: blue">poisoned</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">An <span
style="color: blue">ARP cache</span> is <span
style="color: blue">poisoned</span> somewhere (see slide 25)</span></li>
</ul>
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">For example:</span> a machine on your local
network sees your DNS request for dbs.com.sg and responds quickly with
corrupt information (91.207.5.138). From then on your computer sends all
your network traffic to the corrupt address; your cache is poisoned.</p>
</div>
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Another example: </span> a machine on an
upstream network sees your DNS server’s request for dbs.com.sg and
responds quickly with corrupt information (91.207.5.138). From then on
your computer sends all your network traffic to the corrupt address; the
DNS server’s cache is poisoned.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Platform for IP attacks: Ettercap<span class="hfill-right">
<span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/MITM.mp4">5</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 80%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/ettercap.png" style="height: auto; width:100.0%"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Spoofing, logging, DNS, ARP, DHCP
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Ettercap is a platform for a wide range of network specific attacks -
for example it can easily do any of the attacks we have just seen.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Layer 4 and 7 DoS/DDoS attacks (Sam Bowne/DEFCON)<span
class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/SamBowneLayer4.srt.mp4">7</a>,<a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/SamBowneLayer7.srt.mp4">8</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-bubble"
style="background-color: rgb(128,255,128); width: 80%; margin-left: 0; margin-right: auto;">
<p><span>Sam Bowne explains about (ISO OSI) Layer 4 and 7
attacks.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 89%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="image-grid">
<img data-src="../../images/SamBrowneLayer4.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:4.5cm"
alt="image" />
<img data-src="../../images/SamBrowneLayer7.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:4.5cm"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Another vector: the routers to (re) direct traffic</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
And they are computers...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Since the Internet <span
style="color: blue">topology/connectivity</span> can <span
style="color: blue">change</span>, the routers continually <span
style="color: blue">exchange “routing information”.</span> This is in
line with the original goals of a system which can survive nuclear
attacks on parts of the network.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Routing information is communicated using routing protocols, for
example:</p>
</div>
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">RIP:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>(Routing Information Protocol) an older protocol for exchanging
routing information. Your PC understands RIP, but most routers ignore it
these days. Not all.</p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">IGRP:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>(Interior Gateway Routing Protocol) is a proprietary (CISCO)
protocol, created to overcome limitations of RIP for large networks.</p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">BGP:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>(Border Gateway Protocol) makes most routing decisions on the
Internet.</p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>These <span style="color: blue">protocols update tables/caches</span>
in each connected router to allow it to make decisions where to send IP
traffic. <span style="color: red">These routers themselves can be lied
to (in terms of routing info), and can be hacked.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Summary</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Many opportunities for manipulation:
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your <span
style="color: blue">configuration</span> information could be <span
style="color: blue">altered</span> (your PC gets bad IP addresses, DNS
servers, routers)</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your PC can be convinced to
send your traffic via a hacker’s computer for <span
style="color: blue">man-in-the-middle</span> attacks</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Your ability to <span
style="color: blue">map</span> names to IP addresses, and IP addresses
to hardware MAC addresses can be <span
style="color: blue">manipulated</span> (DNS and ARP
poisoning)</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Resources on a server can be
exhausted to do DoS.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Ongoing <span
style="color: blue">sessions</span> you have initiated (for example to
get a web page) can be <span style="color: blue">hijacked</span> midway
through.</span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Outline</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-toc">
<ol>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Networks (Warning - no mathematics
inside)</span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Sources of insecurity - complex
computer systems</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Basics of Internet
traffic</span></li>
</ul></li>
<li><span style="color: blue"><span class="dada-bold">Attacks and
defences</span></span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Exploration of attacks</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-bold">IP specific defences - IPv6,
TLS...</span></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>TLS<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security">i5</a>,<a
href="https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5246%20">X</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 85%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div
class="table-with-borders table-col-no-left-border table-col-no-right-border table-row-no-top-border table-row-no-bottom-border">
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img data-src="../../images/SSL.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:3cm" alt="image" /></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img
data-src="../../images/1024px-Taher_Elgamal_it-sa_2010.jpeg"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:3cm" alt="image" /></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"> <img
data-src="../../images/ssl_handshake.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:3cm"
alt="image" /></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Transport Layer Security service (originally SSL)
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>SSL was developed by Taher Elgamal at Netscape for it’s browser in
1995. TLS is an IETF standard see <span>[</span>X<span>]</span>
above.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Public key cryptography is used to <span
style="color: blue">authenticate</span>, and negotiate <span
style="color: blue">encryption</span>, for ordinary <span
style="color: blue">symmetric key</span> communication (3-DES, AES...),
with new session keys for each connection.<span style="color: blue">
</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>TLS has problems too<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://web.archive.org/web/20170614120918/https://www.ietf.org/mail-archive/web/tls/current/msg03928.html">ctrl-i6</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Summary/key points...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Standard protocols (HTTP, SMTP ...) are “<span
style="color: blue">tunnelled</span> ” through the SSL layer.<span
style="color: blue"> PKI,PK</span> are used for <span
style="color: blue">authentication</span>, and to <span
style="color: blue">exchange</span> session keys.<span
style="color: blue"> Symmetric</span> protocols (such as 3DES) are used
for encryption.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The protocol includes an idea of not just public/private keys, but
public keys authenticated by a <span style="color: blue">certifying
authority</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
Web no longer secure, civilization will soon collapse...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Unfortunately, in 2009, various attacks on SSL/TLS were discovered,
from <span style="color: blue">loopholes</span> in the <span
style="color: blue">protocol</span>. An attacker is able to <span
style="color: blue">execute any HTTP transaction</span>, authenticated
by a legitimate user</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>You can read about it at <span>[</span>ctrl-i6<span>]</span>
above.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 2px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>It allows a man-in-the-middle to inject a <span
style="color: blue">chosen plaintext prefix</span> into the encrypted
data stream. An “authentication gap” exists during a renegotiation
process during which <span style="color: blue">separate SSL/TLS
connections</span> can be <span style="color: blue">put together</span>.
Fixes were done in early 2010, but <span style="color: blue">many
servers and browsers still not fixed</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>IPsec<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/movies/Intnet2.mp4">i7</a>,<a
href="https://www.faqs.org/rfcs/rfc4301.html">X</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 40%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/IPv4AndIPv6.png" style="height: auto; width:100.0%"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
IPsec is a set of standards:
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Intended to support communication security between networked
computers, particularly in the newer <span
style="color: blue">IPv6</span> (IP Next-Generation) network. IPsec
software is available in Windows, Linux, and on routers on the Internet
(see <span>[</span>XX<span>]</span> above).</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>IPsec may be used in a range of ways, including to provide VPNs
(Virtual Private Networks) flexibly.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Why do we need IPsec VPNs when we already have TLS (and SSH)
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>With TLS/ we set up <span style="color: blue">individual encrypted
links</span> for each application. In a professional environment we may
want <span style="color: blue">end-to-end encryption</span>, where all
traffic is encrypted (not just the https traffic). IPsec can flexibly
set up and tunnel as needed. IPsec takes care to use (high end)
cryptography for all purposes - IPv6 and IPsec are all designed with
security in mind.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Summary: IP networks</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Today, we have seen...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">History</span> - We visited the birth of
the Internet, and saw that its design was primarily driven by resilience
concerns. We looked at it’s growth and complexity.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">IP Protocol weaknesses</span> - We looked
at the layered structure of communication, and saw how both complexity
and a lack of security focus make for a range of issues; attacks on ARP,
the DNS, DHCP, web servers and so on.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">The future</span> - The next generation of
the Internet has a fair amount of security built in, and as we adopt
IPv6 and IPsec more and more, some of these issues will go away.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section></section>
<section id="footnotes" class="footnotes footnotes-end-of-document"
role="doc-endnotes">
<hr />
<ol>
<li id="fn1"><p>This led to facebook changing to https-by-default.<a
href="#/fnref1" class="footnote-back" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p></li>
</ol>
</section>
